course: dictionary
lesson: 12392
title: Adding up over time
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Cumulative means growing bit by bit, because each new amount is added to all the amounts before it. Think of a piggy bank: every week you add a coin, and the total keeps going up.
explain: «Sam saved 2 pounds on Monday, 3 pounds on Tuesday and 4 pounds on Wednesday.» His cumulative savings after Wednesday are 9 pounds, because 2 + 3 + 4 = 9. Each day's total is a running total.
explain: Cumulative can also describe effects that build up slowly. «Too much sun, day after day, has a cumulative effect on your skin.» One day may do little harm, but the damage adds up over time. Check your meaning by swapping it in: "adding up over time" still makes sense, so it fits.
fact: Cumulative means increasing by adding one amount to another over time, so the total keeps growing.
fact: A cumulative total is a running total: each new number is added to all the earlier ones.
fact: «Cumulative» effects build up slowly, like daily practice that adds up to real skill.
fact: A cumulative test covers everything you have learned so far, not just the last topic.
fact: In maths you may meet «cumulative frequency», which is a running total of counts.
exercise: «The cumulative total after three days was 60 points.» What does cumulative mean in this sentence? | A bad mark | A very small number | Only the score from one day | *Running total, adding up each day
exercise: Mia's scores were 4, 6 and 5. What is her cumulative total after the third score? | 20 | 5 | *15 | 11
exercise: Which word means a total that builds up bit by bit? | *Cumulative | Empty | Tiny | Sudden
test: «Reading ten minutes every night has a cumulative effect on your vocabulary.» What does this suggest? | Reading only matters on Sundays | *The benefit builds up slowly over time | Reading once makes you an expert overnight | Reading hurts your eyes
test: A cumulative test covers which lessons? | *Everything learned so far | Only the lessons you enjoyed | None at all | Only the last lesson
exam: Which sentence uses cumulative correctly? | The cumulative of the coin fell off the table | The cumulative was very cold today | *The cumulative total of our points grew each week | Cumulative is a type of dog
